    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Fon kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> plateau in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, founded in the seventeenth century by a branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a>'s dynasty.</li><li>It became a major supplier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> after conquering the port of Whydah in 1727.</li><li>Its women soldiers fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1890–94, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> fell in 1892 and the kingdom was absorbed by 1904.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Dahomey's women soldiers included a unit of 'reapers' armed with long razors; Europeans called the corps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a>, while the Fon called them mino, 'our mothers'.</p></aside>
    </section>

