    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/do-aklin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/do-aklin.jpg" alt="Do-Aklin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/do-aklin">Do-Aklin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Symbole_de_Gangnihessou_roi_du_Dahomey_au_mur_de_la_place_Goho_%C3%A0_Abomey_au_B%C3%A9nin_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adoscam, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Dahomey grew out of the Aja-speaking world of the Slave Coast, in the south of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. According to its royal traditions, a succession dispute in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a> early in the seventeenth century led one branch of the royal family to move north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> plateau, where it settled among the Fon and other local peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/do-aklin">Do-Aklin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakodonou">Dakodonou</a> are remembered as early leaders, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houegbadja">Houegbadja</a>, who probably reigned from around 1645 to 1685, is regarded as the true founder of the kingdom. He built the first royal palace at Abomey, and later tradition derived the name Dahomey, 'in the belly of Dan', from the story of a local chief buried beneath its foundations, a tale historians treat as legend. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akaba">Akaba</a> and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">Agaja</a>, who came to the throne in 1708 or 1718 according to different chronologies, Dahomey turned from an inland state into a power on the coast. Agaja conquered Allada in 1724 and in 1727 the Kingdom of Whydah, the busiest slave-trading port of the region, giving Dahomey direct access to European traders. These conquests provoked repeated invasions by the cavalry of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>, and around 1730 Agaja accepted tributary status under Oyo, a relationship that lasted almost a century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abomey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abomey.jpg" alt="Abomey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rue_d%27Abomey_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ji-Elle, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dahomey's economy combined intensive farming on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> plateau with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>. Farmers grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, yams, beans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-palm">oil palm</a> supplied food and, later, the main export. In the eighteenth century the kingdom became one of the largest African suppliers of the Atlantic slave trade, selling war captives through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a>, as Europeans called Whydah, to French, English, Portuguese and Brazilian merchants. The crown claimed priority in the trade, collected customs at the port through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, the <em>yovogan</em>, and received firearms, cloth, tobacco, alcohol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowrie</a> shells, which served as currency. Brazilian merchants such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-felix-de-souza">Francisco Félix de Souza</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Chacha</a>, became powerful intermediaries in the early nineteenth century. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> pressed for an end to the slave trade, Dahomey adapted by expanding oil palm plantations, worked largely by enslaved labour, and exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> from the 1840s. Religion was based on the <em>vodun</em>, deities of nature and of the ancestors served by priests and initiates, and on Fa divination; the royal ancestors received particular honour. Craftsmen of Abomey, organised in families attached to the court, produced appliqué cloths, cast brass figures, iron sculpture and painted clay bas-reliefs that recorded royal victories on the palace walls.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ghezo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghezo.jpg" alt="Ghezo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gezo_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Forbes, Frederick E., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dahomey was among the most militarised states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. War was waged almost every year in the dry season, and its aims were both political and economic, since captives supplied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a> and the sacrifices of royal ceremonies. The army combined a standing core of royal troops with general levies, and by the eighteenth century it relied heavily on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> bought from Europeans. Its most famous feature was a corps of women soldiers, called <em>ahosi</em>, 'king's wives', or <em>mino</em>, 'our mothers', and known to Europeans as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahomey-amazons">Dahomey Amazons</a>. Its origins are debated and may lie in royal bodyguards or elephant hunters of the early eighteenth century, but under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> it was expanded into a regular force of several thousand, organised in units of musketeers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and 'reapers' armed with long razors. Dahomey's armies destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a> and Whydah, raided the Mahi to the north and the Yoruba-speaking towns to the east, and in 1886 sacked the Kingdom of Ketu. They also suffered heavy defeats: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>'s cavalry invaded repeatedly in the 1720s, and the Egba stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a> repulsed Dahomean assaults in 1851 and 1864 with severe losses. Against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1890–1894 the men and women of Dahomey fought with great determination, but repeating rifles and artillery prevailed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ouidah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ouidah.jpg" alt="Ouidah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Door_of_no_return.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco Anzola, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dahomey was one of the most centralised kingdoms of pre-colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The king, normally chosen by his predecessor from among the royal sons, held authority over land, trade, war and life and death, and princes were excluded from the highest offices to limit rivalry. The leading ministers were the <em>migan</em>, responsible for justice and executions, and the <em>mehu</em>, who managed finances, palace affairs and the princes, while the yovogan governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a> and dealt with Europeans. Each male official had a female counterpart among the palace women, who kept watch on him and reported to the king, a system examined by Edna Bay in her study of royal wives. The palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> housed thousands of women, from royal wives and soldiers to officials and servants, and the queen mother, the <em>kpojito</em>, was a powerful institution. Royal power was renewed in the Annual Customs, ceremonies honouring the royal ancestors at which the king displayed his wealth, received tribute, distributed gifts and offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifices</a>, whose scale European visitors often exaggerated but which were real. Later accounts describe a regular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of population and resources kept with tallies of pebbles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> overthrew his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adandozan">Adandozan</a> in 1818, whose name was then struck from the official king list, and ended tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> around 1823.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey.jpg" alt="Royal Palaces of Abomey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey">Royal Palaces of Abomey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abomey_2006_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joachim Huber, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dahomey's history is inseparable from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, in which it was one of the major African participants during the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Hundreds of thousands of people, many of them captives of Dahomean wars, left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a> and neighbouring ports, and their religious traditions shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>: the <em>vodun</em> of the Fon and their neighbours is a principal root of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tambor-de-mina">Tambor de Mina</a> tradition of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. The kingdom's ties with Brazil were especially close, and returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agudas">Afro-Brazilians</a> formed a distinct community on the coast. Dahomey's centralised government, royal ceremonies and women soldiers fascinated European observers, and the anthropologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melville-herskovits">Melville Herskovits</a> and the economic historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-polanyi">Karl Polanyi</a> made it a classic case in debates about African states and economies. Its art is among the best known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey">Royal Palaces of Abomey</a>, with their painted bas-reliefs, were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1985, and its iron sculpture and royal statues are celebrated works of African art. In 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> twenty-six royal treasures taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> by French troops in 1892. The memory of the kingdom, and especially of its women soldiers, remains central to the national identity of Benin.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-franco-dahomean-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-franco-dahomean-war.jpg" alt="First Franco-Dahomean War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-franco-dahomean-war">First Franco-Dahomean War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dahomey_amazon2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dahomey's decline began with the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, which eroded the economic foundation of royal power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1852 promising to end slave exports but continued the trade where he could, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>, produced by many small farmers, spread wealth beyond the court's control. Defeats before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a> in 1851 and 1864 damaged the army's prestige, and in the 1860s the slave trade to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> came to an end. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glele">Glele</a> (r. 1858–1889) the kingdom faced growing French influence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a> and disputes over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotonou">Cotonou</a>, which Dahomey had ceded in treaties of uncertain validity. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behanzin">Behanzin</a>, who succeeded in 1889, rejected French claims, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-franco-dahomean-war">First Franco-Dahomean War</a> of 1890 ended in a treaty that recognised French positions on the coast. In 1892 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sent a column of some two thousand men under Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-amedee-dodds">Alfred-Amédée Dodds</a>, which defeated the Dahomean army and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> in November 1892 as Behanzin withdrew. Behanzin surrendered in January 1894 and was exiled to Martinique, dying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1906. France installed his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoli-agbo">Agoli-agbo</a> as king of a reduced Abomey but deposed and exiled him in 1900, and by 1904 the former kingdom had been fully absorbed into the colony of French Dahomey within French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. The colony became independent in 1960 and took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> in 1975.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Dahomey built one of the most centralised and militarised states of pre-colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> plateau, profited heavily from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a>, fielded a famous corps of women soldiers and left palaces, art and religious traditions that shaped both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</em></p>

